position. The offline-constructed tuple δ⋆(t) forms the\nuniversal intermittent template and remains fixed across test\nrecordings and target LALMs.\n\nDistributional Requery Guard\nDistributional Requery Guard (DRG) is a plug-in input filter\nthat detects low-frequency distribution shifts before LALM\ninference and requests independent acoustic evidence only\nfor flagged inputs. Offline, we construct a detector training\nset Ddef = Dcle ∪ Djam, where Dcle contains clean train-\ning recordings and Djam contains training recordings with\nlow-frequency interference. For each defense training record-\ning xdef ∈ Ddef , we apply the short-time Fourier transform\nspecified in Section , sum the resulting magnitudes over time,\nand apply ℓ1 normalization across frequency bins. This op-\neration produces a spectral distribution descriptor dx ∈ RV ,\nwhose component dx[v] represents the relative magnitude\nin frequency bin v. The normalization reduces sensitivity\nto overall recording amplitude while preserving the spectral\ndistribution used for detection.\n\nWe cluster the training descriptors into two groups with\nK-means. Let G1 and G2 denote the resulting sets of training\nrecordings, and let µk ∈ RV denote the centroid of clus-\nter Gk. Both fitting and assignment use Euclidean distance.\nTo determine which cluster represents interference, we com-\npute its mean descriptor mass within the same low-frequency\nrange [fmin, fmax] used to construct ILL:\n\nrk =\n\n1\n|Gk|\n\n(cid:88)\n\n(cid:88)\n\nx∈Gk\n\nv: νv∈[fmin,fmax]\n\ndx[v],\n\nk ∈ {1, 2}.\n\n(12)\n\nWe label the cluster with the larger mass as the interference\ncluster, kjam = arg maxk∈{1,2} rk, and label the remaining\ncluster as kcle = 3 − kjam. This procedure depends only on\nthe input spectra and does not query the target LALM.\n\nAt deployment, DRG extracts the descriptor of the user’s\ninitial recording x(1) and assigns it to the nearest centroid.\nA recording assigned to kcle follows the standard inference\npath. For a recording assigned to kjam, the system requests\na second recording x(2) of the same utterance and supplies\nboth recordings to the target LALM with an instruction to\ncompare them and answer only from content that is consis-\ntent and intelligible across the pair. The second recording\ntherefore provides independent acoustic evidence without\ndiscarding usable content from the initial recording. Given\ndx, DRG adds only two V -dimensional distance evaluations,\nyielding O(V ) detection time without a neural forward pass\nor target-LALM query; Appendix B provides the full com-\nplexity analysis.\n\nExperiments\n\nExperimental Setup\nModels. We evaluate four open-weight LALMs: MiniCPM-\nal. 2026), StepAudio2\net\no 4.5 (MiniCPM)\n\n(Cui\n\nFigure 2: Attack effectiveness on translation and recognition.\nLower BLEU and higher WER indicate stronger disruption.\n\n(StepAudio2)\n\nMini\n(Wu et al. 2025), Qwen2.5-Omni\n(Qwen2.5) (Xu et al. 2025a), and Qwen3-Omni (Qwen3) (Xu\net al. 2025b). We also test two closed-source LALMs, GPT-\naudio-mini (GPT) and Gemini-3.5-flash (Gemini). We con-\nstruct ILL once with Qwen2.5-Omni as the reference model\nand transfer the fixed waveform to all other targets.\n\nTasks and Datasets. We use MMAU’s speech, sound,\nand music categories (Sakshi et al. 2025) for audio ques-\ntion answering, LibriSpeech (Panayotov et al. 2015) for\nspeech recognition, English-to-Chinese CoVoST 2 (Wang\net al. 2021) for speech translation, and RAVDESS (Living-\nstone and Russo 2018) for emotion classification. We sample\n100 test examples per dataset and construct ILL using another\ndisjoint set of 100 examples.\n\nAttack Baselines. We compare ILL with four attacks.\nGaussian substitutes random Gaussian noise (Franceschi,\nFawzi, and Fawzi 2018) under ILL’s duty cycle. PNL se-\nlects one common interference recording from each of three\n115 Noise categories: natural, mechanical, and human (Ko\net al. 2017). Audio-Adv is a targeted white-box attack that in-\nduces chosen DeepSpeech transcriptions (Carlini and Wag-\nner 2018). Whisper is a universal acoustic attack that sup-\npresses Whisper transcription (Raina et al. 2024). Clean and\nattacked conditions use paired source audio and matched\nbudgets where applicable.\n\nDefense Baselines. We compare DRG with direct low-\nfrequency suppression (LF-Suppression), Deep Feature Loss\n(DFL) speech denoising (Germain, Chen, and Koltun 2018),\nand MMSE-STSA enhancement (Ephraim and Malah 1984).\nMetrics and Evaluation Protocol. We report accuracy for\nMMAU and RAVDESS. LibriSpeech uses WER, whereas\nCoVoST 2 uses BLEU (Papineni et al. 2002). We assess\nobjective stealthiness with the audible noise ratio defined in\nSection ; lower values indicate that less perturbation energy\nfalls in the nominal audible band. Subjective stealthiness\nuses human ratings from 1 to 7, where lower values indicate\nlower perceived audibility. Defense detection uses precision,\nrecall, and F1, while recovery uses the corresponding end-\ntask metric after requery.\n\nAttack Effectiveness\nTable 1 and Figure 2 show that the fixed ILL waveform\ndegrades all four task types. This effect does not weaken\nafter black-box transfer: the largest RAVDESS reduction, 67\npercentage points, occurs on the unseen StepAudio2 target\nrather than on the Qwen2.5 reference model. The consistent\n\nQwen2.5Qwen3MiniCPMStepAudioGPTGemini0.00.20.4BLEU ↑CoVoST2Qwen2.5Qwen3MiniCPMStepAudioGPTGemini012WER ↓LibriSpeechCleanBaseline avg.ILL (Our)GaussianPNLAudio-Adv.Whisper\fTable 1: Attack results on accuracy-based tasks. MMAU scores are averaged over its speech, sound, and music categories.\nSubscripts report the accuracy change relative to the clean setting in percentage points (pp): gray denotes drops of at most 10\npp (including no change), red denotes drops greater than 10 pp, and green denotes gains.\n\nModel\n\nDataset\n\nQwen2.5\n\nQwen3\n\nMiniCPM\n\nStepAudio\n\nGPT\n\nGemini\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nMMAU\nRAVDESS\n\nClean\n\n73.3%\n47.0%\n\n74.7%\n29.0%\n\n71.7%\n18.0%\n\n70.0%\n75.0%\n\n61.0%\n9.0%\n\n73.7%\n40.0%\n\nGaussian\n\n61.3% 12%\n12.0% 35%\n\n57.3% 17%\n12.0% 17%\n\n61.0% 11%\n9.0% 9%\n\n60.7% 9%\n12.0% 63%\n\n51.0% 10%\n2.0% 7%\n\n61.7% 12%\n17.0% 23%\n\nPNL\n\nAudio-Adv.\n\n60.9% 12%\n11.3% 36%\n\n60.6% 14%\n11.0% 18%\n\n60.9% 11%\n9.3% 9%\n\n58.4% 12%\n11.3% 64%\n\n54.0% 7%\n3.3% 6%\n\n58.7% 15%\n13.3% 27%\n\n69.3% 4%\n14.0% 33%\n\n69.3% 5%\n14.0% 15%\n\n69.3% 2%\n9.0% 9%\n\n67.3% 3%\n32.0% 43%\n\n58.3% 3%\n2.0% 7%\n\n71.0% 3%\n27.0% 13%\n\nWhisper\n\n72.3% 1%\n33.0% 14%\n\n71.7% 3%\n30.0% 1%\n\n71.0% 1%\n13.0% 5%\n\n68.0% 2%\n53.0% 22%\n\n60.7% 0%\n4.0% 5%\n\n69.7% 4%\n38.0% 2%\n\nILL (Our)\n\n61.3% 12%\n8.0% 39%\n\n59.3% 15%\n8.0% 21%\n\n60.3% 11%\n10.0% 8%\n\n56.3% 14%\n8.0% 67%\n\n50.0% 11%\n0.0% 9%\n\n58.3% 15%\n12.0% 28%\n\nTable 2: Audible noise ratio (ANR, %) for clean audio and seven interference methods across four datasets.\n\nDataset\n\nMMAU\nLibriSpeech\nCoVoST2\nRAVDESS\n\nClean\n\n0.00%\n0.00%\n0.00%\n0.00%\n\nGaussian\n\nPNL (Natural)\n\nPNL (Machine)\n\nPNL (Human)\n\nAudio-Adv.\n\nWhisper\n\nILL (Our)\n\n99.63%\n99.63%\n99.63%\n99.62%\n\n100.00%\n100.00%\n100.00%\n100.00%\n\n99.69%\n99.65%\n99.67%\n99.68%\n\n100.00%\n100.00%\n100.00%\n99.99%\n\n98.94%\n98.94%\n98.94%\n98.94%\n\n98.93%\n98.93%\n98.93%\n98.93%\n\n0.08%\n0.07%\n0.07%\n0.06%\n\ndegradation on the other five LALMs therefore indicates that\nILL has strong migration capabilities.\n\nILL does not uniformly outperform every audible-noise\nbaseline, but it achieves comparable and, in some settings,\nstronger disruption using only the 5–20 Hz band. It gives\nthe lowest CoVoST 2 BLEU on every target in Figure 2\nand remains competitive on the accuracy-based tasks. The\nkey finding is therefore not uniform numerical dominance,\nbut that a low-perceptibility frequency band alone can cause\nbroad degradation across LALMs.\n\nAcoustic Stealthiness\nTable 2 reports the proportion of perturbation energy that\nfalls in the measured audible band. Human hearing is\nconventionally characterized over approximately 20 Hz–\n20 kHz (Salt and Hullar 2010). Because ILL operates at\n5–20 Hz, we quantify this spectral leakage as:\n\nANR = 100 ×\n\nEnoise(20 Hz–8 kHz)\nEnoise(full band)\n\n,\n\n(13)\n\nwhere lower ANR indicates less perturbation energy in this\nband. Across the six datasets, ILL has an ANR of only 0.06–\n0.08%, whereas all evaluated baselines exceed 98.9%. ILL\ntherefore achieves its task-level effect with negligible spectral\nleakage into the measured audible band.\n\nWe complement ANR with 112 complete human response\nsets covering clean audio and all seven interference methods.\nFigure 3 shows that ILL receives a mean rating of 1.33 and\na median of 1, close to the clean condition with a mean of\n1.17 and a median of 1. Every comparison method receives\na higher mean rating; the closest are Audio-Adv. at 3.75\n\nFigure 3: Human audibility ratings across 100+ responses.\n\nand Whisper at 3.92. The agreement between the human\nratings and ANR supports the low perceptibility of ILL in\nthis evaluation without treating spectral leakage alone as\nevidence of human imperceptibility.\n\nDefense Evaluation\nDRG first identifies whether the input contains low-frequency\ninterference. As shown in Table 3, the Average training con-\ndition yields F1 scores of 89.69–99.00% across the four eval-\nuation datasets. The result indicates that the spectral distri-\nbution provides a reliable trigger for conditional requery.\n\nWe then evaluate recovery under two realistic conditions.\nA clean second recording represents transient interference,\nwhereas a noisy second recording represents persistent in-\nterference. As shown in Figure 4, DRG raises the six-model\nmean accuracy from 28.5% without defense to 46.1% af-\n\nCleanGaussianPNL (Natural)PNL (Machine)PNL (Human)Audio-Adv.WhisperILL (Our)1234567Human Rating ↓\fTable 3: Precision, recall, and F1 scores (%) for DRG inter-\nference detection under different detector training datasets.\n\nTable 4: Benign task performance under standard clean in-\nference and forced DRG requery.\n\nTraining dataset Metric\n\nMMAU LibriSpeech CoVoST2 RAVDESS\n\nDataset\n\nMiniCPM\n\nStepAudio\n\nQwen3\n\nLibriSpeech\n\nCoVoST2\n\nAverage\n\nPrecision\nRecall\nF1-score\n\nPrecision\nRecall\nF1-score\n\nPrecision\nRecall\nF1-score\n\n93.40\n99.00\n96.12\n\n88.24\n88.24\n89.11\n\n99.00\n99.00\n99.00\n\n93.46\n100.00\n96.62\n\n85.19\n85.19\n88.46\n\n97.98\n97.00\n97.49\n\n88.50\n100.00\n93.90\n\n86.41\n86.41\n87.68\n\n100.00\n97.00\n98.48\n\n59.17\n100.00\n74.35\n\n60.24\n60.24\n75.19\n\n81.30\n100.00\n89.69\n\nClean\n\nDRG\n\nClean\n\nDRG\n\nClean\n\nDRG\n\nLibriSpeech W ER↓\nCoVoST2 BLEU ↑\nMMAU ACC↑\nRAVDESS ACC↑\n\n0.0348\n0.4382\n72.0%\n18.0%\n\n0.0374\n0.4268\n69.0%\n21.0%\n\n0.0298\n0.4566\n66.0%\n75.0%\n\n0.0309\n0.4455\n69.0%\n74.0%\n\n0.0320\n0.4852\n75.0%\n29.0%\n\n0.0370\n0.4839\n62.0%\n25.0%\n\nFigure 4: Defense effectiveness of DRG.\n\nter clean reacquisition, outperforming the signal-processing\nbaselines. It also remains above the no-defense mean under\npersistent interference, although the smaller gain confirms\nthat recovery depends on the quality of the second recording.\nWe also test the same recovery procedure on other attack\ntypes. Figure 5 shows that clean reacquisition is best or tied\nfor best in 19 of 24 settings. This result indicates that DRG’s\nrequery mechanism can recover useful semantic evidence\nbeyond the low-frequency interference used in its design.\n\nBenign Utility\nTable 4 reports a conservative stress test in which DRG is\nforced to requery every clean input. Ten of the twelve scores\nremain within 0.03 of standard inference, and DRG exceeds\nstandard inference on two classification results. The utility\nchange is therefore small in most evaluated settings.\n\nAnalysis\nThis section isolates which design choices make ILL effec-\ntive and examines model-internal evidence for the observed\nfailures. Unless otherwise stated, controlled ablations use\nQwen3-Omni, while mechanistic comparisons retain paired\nclean, attacked, and defended inputs.\n\nAblation Studies\nFigure 6 tests whether ILL depends on a particular operating\npoint or on its structured low-frequency construction. Lower\ntask performance indicates a stronger attack in all four panels.\n\nFigure 5: Recovery under four additional attack types.\n\nPerturbation Amplitude. Figure 6(a) shows a steep per-\nformance decline up to β = 4, followed by saturation. We\nselect this knee point because it captures most of the attack\ngain without using the largest tested amplitude.\n\nDuty Cycle. Figure 6(b) shows that attack effectiveness\nimproves as the active proportion increases, but the curve\nchanges little beyond γ > 70%. The selected duty cycle\ntherefore reaches the saturation region while leaving part of\nthe waveform inactive.\n\nAttack Duration. Figure 6(c) reveals a clear duration\nthreshold at the default segment length. Shorter segments\nare ineffective, whereas extending the segment by one or two\norders of magnitude provides no further gain. The default\nlength is therefore sufficient to trigger the observed failure.\nState-Sequence Construction. Figure 6(d) shows that\nILL causes greater degradation than Gaussian noise, a fixed-\nfrequency signal, and a uniform frequency sweep on both\nclassification and translation. This consistent advantage sup-\nports the effectiveness of the proposed state-sequence design.\n\nTemporal Segmentation Consistency.\nTable 5 examines whether Sentence Attention Scale Esti-\nmation yields comparable active-interval durations across\ndatasets and reference LALMs. All estimates lie between\n3.67 and 5.07 seconds, and the largest cross-model spread\nwithin a dataset is 0.50 seconds. This consistency supports\nusing a corpus-level attention statistic to determine the ILL\nschedule rather than relying on model-specific timing.\n\nAttention and Representation Shift\nTable 6 shows that I", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
